  ---
  name: early-withdrawal-exceptions
  description: Use when the user is under 59½ and asks how to avoid the 10% additional tax (often called the early withdrawal penalty), or asks about emergency, domestic abuse, birth or adoption, disaster, long-term care, terminal illness, Rule of 55, disability, or other exceptions, including repaying a withdrawal later.
  ---
  # Early Withdrawal Exceptions

  Find out whether a withdrawal before age 59½ can avoid the 10% additional federal tax, and explain the limits and repayment rights. These exceptions remove only the 10% additional tax. The withdrawal is still taxed as ordinary income, unless it is a qualified Roth distribution.

  ## Procedure

  1. Confirm the user's age (or the year they turn 55 or 59½) and whether they are still employed by the plan sponsor.
  2. Ask what the money is for, at the level of a category only. Match it to the tables below.
  3. Check plan-rules to see how the money can come out of the plan:
     - If the plan offers the SECURE 2.0 distribution type, the user can request it while employed.
     - If it doesn't, the exception can still apply to a withdrawal the plan otherwise allows, such as a hardship withdrawal or a withdrawal after leaving. The user claims it on their tax return. Repayment can go to an IRA if the plan won't accept it.
  4. Explain the limit, whether it can be repaid, and what the user must certify. Then offer the withdrawal-tax-estimator for net cash.
  5. Mention state rules: some states have their own early distribution tax (for example, California charges an additional 2.5%).

  ## SECURE 2.0 distributions

  | Type | Limit | Who qualifies | Repayment |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | Emergency personal expense | One per calendar year, up to the lesser of $1,000 or the vested balance above $1,000 | Self-certified unforeseeable or immediate need for personal or family emergency expenses. No documentation needed | Can repay within 3 years. No further emergency distribution in the next 3 calendar years unless it is repaid or new contributions equal the amount |
  | Domestic abuse victim | The lesser of $10,500 (2026 limit, indexed each year; it was $10,300 in 2025) or 50% of the vested balance | Self-certified abuse by a spouse or domestic partner within the past year. Abuse includes physical, sexual, emotional, psychological, and economic abuse | Can repay within 3 years |
  | Qualified birth or adoption | Up to $5,000 per child, per parent | Taken within 1 year after the birth or finalized adoption. The child's name and taxpayer ID go on the tax return | Can repay within 3 years |
  | Qualified disaster recovery | Up to $22,000 per disaster | Principal residence in a federally declared disaster area and an economic loss, within the official window. Reported on Form 8915-F | Can repay within 3 years. Income can be spread over 3 tax years |
  | Qualified long-term care | The lesser of premiums paid, 10% of the vested balance, or an indexed dollar cap ($2,500 base amount; confirm the current-year figure). Available for distributions after December 29, 2025 | Pays premiums for certified long-term care insurance, with a premium statement | Cannot be repaid |

  ## Tax exceptions that apply to an otherwise allowed withdrawal

  | Exception | Key condition |
  | --- | --- |
  | Rule of 55 | Left the employer in or after the calendar year they turn 55. Applies only to that employer's plan. Rolling the money into an IRA loses this exception. Qualified public safety employees, mainly in government plans but also private-sector firefighters, can use age 50 or 25 years of service, whichever comes first |
  | Terminal illness | A physician certifies an illness expected to result in death within 84 months. No dollar cap. Can repay within 3 years |
  | Total and permanent disability | Unable to do any substantial gainful activity, expected to be long-lasting or to result in death |
  | Medical expenses | Only the unreimbursed medical expenses above 7.5% of adjusted gross income, paid in the same year as the withdrawal |
  | Substantially equal periodic payments | A series of payments after leaving the employer, under IRS rules. Changing the series before the later of 5 years or age 59½ brings back the 10% additional tax on all earlier payments, plus interest |
  | Court-ordered division (QDRO) | Paid to an alternate payee, such as an ex-spouse |
  | Qualified reservist | Called to active duty for more than 179 days. The withdrawal must be made during active duty and can be repaid to an IRA within 2 years after it ends |
  | IRS levy | Taken by the IRS to pay a tax debt |
  | Death | Paid to a beneficiary |

  **IRA-only exceptions do not apply to a 401(k).** The first-time homebuyer, higher education, and health-insurance-while-unemployed exceptions apply only to IRAs. Users often confuse these.

  ## How to respond

  - Lead with the best fit for the user's situation, not the whole table.
  - For domestic abuse, be brief and kind. Do not ask about the abuse. Explain the option, note it is self-certified and handled confidentially, and share the support contacts from plan-rules.
  - For terminal illness, be gentle and practical. Mention that the withdrawal still needs a permitted reason under the plan, such as hardship, an in-service withdrawal, or leaving the employer.
  - If no exception applies, say so clearly and show the cost of the 10% additional tax with the withdrawal-tax-estimator. Then compare with a loan.

  ## Example

  User: "I'm 56 and leaving next month. Can I take money out without the 10% penalty?"

  - They leave in the year they turn 56, so the Rule of 55 applies to withdrawals from this plan.
  - The withdrawal is still taxable income. A payment to them has 20% federal withholding because it could have been rolled over.
  - Warn that rolling everything to an IRA first would lose the exception for that money until 59½.
